  "summary": "Validates `CFeeRate` values during deserialization by throwing on representations that constructors cannot produce. It specifically rejects streams encoding negative sizes or a zero size paired with a non-zero fee.",
  "problem": "`CFeeRate` deserialization reads fee and size values without the normalization and checks enforced by its constructors. Invalid values like negative sizes can trigger an `Assume` assertion failure in debug builds or lead to incorrect fee calculations in release builds when evaluated by `FeeFrac::EvaluateFee`. While unused on master today, IPC interfaces such as #29409 pass feerates across process boundaries as serialized data.",
  "evidence": [
    "Author description and commit message note that unchecked deserialization allows negative sizes into FeeFrac::EvaluateFee, causing 'an Assume failure' in debug builds and 'wrong fees' in release builds",
    "PR notes the issue was discovered by Project Loupe and passed on by Greg Sanders"
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [],
    "enables": [
      "#29409",
      "#10102"
    ]
  },
  "scope_notes": "Small patch adding a deserialization check in src/policy/feerate.h along with unit test coverage.",
  "changed_since_previous": "",
  "card": "Validates CFeeRate data during deserialization, throwing an error on negative sizes or zero sizes paired with non-zero fees. Unchecked deserialization allows malformed values that trigger assertion failures in debug builds or cause incorrect fee evaluations in release builds. Although master does not deserialize CFeeRate directly, the pending IPC chain interface (#29409) for multiprocess Bitcoin (#10102) passes feerates as serialized byte streams. The issue was discovered by Project Loupe.",
